%N A115019 Position (starting at 0 with the initial 2) of the first block of n consecutive decimal digits of e whose integer value is prime, allowing leading zeros.
%C A115019 a(10)=99 leads to the 10-digit prime 7427466391 which is the answer to the so-called "google puzzle" (January 2006).
%t A115019 With[{digits = First[RealDigits[E, 10, 1000]]}, Table[SelectFirst[Range[0, Length[digits] - n], PrimeQ[FromDigits[Take[digits, # + {1, n}]]] &], {n, 69}]] (* _Eric W. Weisstein_, Oct 05 2026 *)
%o A115019 (PARI) \p10000 e=exp(1): f(i,l)=lift(Mod(floor(e*10^(l-1+i)),10^l)) prem(l)=local(i);i=0;while(!isprime(f(i,l)),i++);i for(l=1,1000,write(indices,Str(prem(l) ", ")))
%Y A115019 Cf. A272304.
